\documentclass{ieee}

\title{On the Convergence of \math{k}-Sparse Attention}

\author{name=Ada Lovelace, affiliation=Analytical Engine Lab, email=ada@example.org}
\author{name=Alan Turing, affiliation=Bletchley Institute, email=alan@example.org}

\date{March 2026}

\begin{abstract}
  We prove that \math{k}-sparse attention converges to full attention as \math{k}
  approaches the sequence length, and bound the error for finite \math{k}.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{attention, sparsity, convergence}

\section{Introduction}

Attention costs \math{O(n^2)} in the sequence length \math{n} \cite{vaswani2017}.
Keeping the top \math{k} scores per query reduces this to \math{O(nk)}, a rule that
has worked well in practice\footnote{See \href{https://example.org}{the benchmarks}.}
but has lacked a simple analysis.

\section{Analysis}

\subsection{Setup}

Let \math{a \in \mathbb{R}^n} be an attention distribution. Its \math{k}-sparse
approximation \math{\tilde{a}} keeps the \math{k} largest entries and renormalizes.

\begin{equation}
  \| \tilde{a} - a \|_1 \le 2 \sum_{j \notin \mathcal{T}} a_j
\end{equation}

\subsection{The bound in practice}

The discarded mass falls quickly as \math{k} grows, as Table 1 shows.

\begin{table}[l, r, r]
  \caption{Discarded attention mass by \math{k}.}
  \head{Dataset}{\math{k = 8}}{\math{k = 32}}
  \row{LongBench}{0.041}{0.006}
  \row{WikiText}{0.058}{0.011}
\end{table}

A short implementation fits in a few lines:

\begin{code}[python]
def sparsify(scores, k):
    top, index = scores.topk(k, dim=-1)
    return top.softmax(dim=-1), index
\end{code}

\section{Conclusion}

Sparse attention is safe whenever the discarded mass is small, which is the common
case. \textit{Peaked} distributions benefit the most.

